---
title: "Install on Linux, desktop or headless"
description: Download KoboldCpp on Linux, make it executable and run it, with notes on the launcher, file pickers and headless servers.
sidebar:
  label: "Linux"
  order: 3
---

KoboldCpp for Linux is a single file. There is no installer and no package to add.

{% steps %}

1. Pick your file. See [Download KoboldCpp](/download/) if you are unsure.

   | Your PC | File |
   |---|---|
   | NVIDIA graphics card | [`koboldcpp-linux-x64`](https://github.com/LostRuins/koboldcpp/releases/latest/download/koboldcpp-linux-x64) |
   | AMD, Intel or no graphics card | [`koboldcpp-linux-x64-nocuda`](https://github.com/LostRuins/koboldcpp/releases/latest/download/koboldcpp-linux-x64-nocuda) |
   | Older processor or older NVIDIA card | [`koboldcpp-linux-x64-oldpc`](https://github.com/LostRuins/koboldcpp/releases/latest/download/koboldcpp-linux-x64-oldpc) |

2. Download it with your browser. It usually lands in `~/Downloads`.

3. In a terminal, go to the folder with the file, make it executable and run it. This example uses the NVIDIA file; replace the name with yours:

   ```sh {% title="Terminal" frame="terminal" %}
   cd ~/Downloads
   chmod +x koboldcpp-linux-x64
   ./koboldcpp-linux-x64
   ```

   The launcher opens.

{% /steps %}

Continue with [Choose a model](/getting-started/model/).

To download from the terminal instead, for example on a server:

```sh {% title="Terminal" frame="terminal" %}
curl -fLO https://github.com/LostRuins/koboldcpp/releases/latest/download/koboldcpp-linux-x64
```

"Permission denied" means the file is not executable yet; run `chmod +x` on it. If the main build fails on an older system, try `koboldcpp-linux-x64-oldpc`, the build with the greatest compatibility.

## Programs the launcher uses

| Program | Needed for |
|---|---|
| `yad` or `zenity` | A better file picker. `yad` is used if both are installed; without either, the launcher uses its built-in picker. |
| `vulkan-tools` | Detecting your graphics card and its memory with `vulkaninfo`. NVIDIA cards are also detected with `nvidia-smi`, and AMD cards with `rocminfo` if ROCm is installed. If none of these works, you see "Unable to detect VRAM." |
| `xdg-open` | Opening the browser after the model has loaded. |

## Launcher problems

- **The file picker does nothing (often on Wayland).** Install `yad`, or turn on **Use Classic FilePicker** on the launcher's **Extra** tab. The console may show `Zenity/YAD sanity check failed`.
- **"Warning, GUI failed to start".** The launcher could not open a window, for example because there is no display. KoboldCpp then tells you to use the command line. See [Headless use](#headless-use).
- **You want to see the console output.** **Spawn Terminal** on the **Extra** tab opens an `xterm` window that mirrors it.

More: [OS-specific problems](/docs/troubleshooting/os-specific/#linux-the-file-picker-does-nothing).

## AMD graphics cards

Start with the **Vulkan** backend in `koboldcpp-linux-x64-nocuda`. Install `vulkan-tools` first, so the launcher can detect your card and select **Use Vulkan** by itself. Without it, KoboldCpp does not find the card and runs on the CPU.

If generation is slow with Vulkan, try the experimental official ROCm build. It does not need ROCm installed. See [ROCm build for AMD on Linux](/docs/install/releases/#advanced-rocm-build-for-amd-on-linux) and [GPU backends: ROCm](/docs/hardware/backends/#rocm).

## Headless use

With a model on the command line, KoboldCpp skips the launcher and needs no display:

```sh {% title="Terminal" frame="terminal" %}
./koboldcpp-linux-x64 --model mymodel.gguf
```

- `./koboldcpp-linux-x64 --help` lists all flags.
- `--config mysettings.kcpps` starts with a saved configuration. A config that contains a model also skips the launcher.
- `--skiplauncher` overrides `--showgui`, including a `showgui` saved in a config. It does not pick a model: without a model from the command line or a config, KoboldCpp exits with an error.
- By default KoboldCpp listens on all network interfaces, so other devices on your network can reach it. Add `--host 127.0.0.1` to allow connections from this machine only.

More: [Command line](/docs/settings/command-line/), [Remote access](/docs/api/remote-access/), [Passwords and security](/docs/api/passwords-and-security/).

## Advanced: ARM and other systems

There is no ready-made file for ARM Linux. [Build from source](/docs/install/from-source/); `koboldcpp.sh` supports aarch64.

The x64 files need glibc 2.29 or newer, for example Ubuntu 20.04 or newer. `ldd --version` shows your glibc version.

## Advanced: unofficial packages

The community maintains packages that are unofficial and get no official support: `koboldcpp-cuda` and `koboldcpp-hipblas` in the AUR, and `koboldcpp` in Nixpkgs.

